I want to start by saying first  congratulations on the show I cannot  wait I've seen the first three episodes  I cannot wait to watch more one of the  things that early on there was a lot of  talk with the Apple shows that they were  going to be very family-friendly and  it's all PG and I can assure people with  this up with this show that this is a  rated R show this is for you know I  would say there's more for adults can  you sort of talk about that aspect that  it's not just aimed at you know this has  more to it you know I would say not  quite are but it would certainly be you  know over over ten but life is that way  so I don't think we're presenting  anything that life doesn't give and  sometimes young children are having to  deal with life and it is interestingly  enough it is absolutely family oriented  because it it's about family all kind of  things happen in family and so we are  looking at in a realistic way especially  when family has survived an apocalyptic  event and it with that that far into the  future so I think you can watch it  certainly with you know you know 11-year  olds who have who you don't hide things  from for both of you one of the things  that really struck me for the show is  the level of production design the world  building it's tremendous can you sort of  talk about that aspect of the show and  what it was like walking on set for the  first time it was beautiful my first day  on set was outside of the cave with the  rain falling and just being in the  elements with my tribe behind me in  Paris in the front and it was really  just being on the land with the people  there was a kind of instinctual sense  that there is something right about it  and natural about it and that's what I  really loved about the show is that it's  this return to the land and to really  the bare bones of human nature and how  we interact with each other  Steven Knight's imagination  is limitless and it is explosive and  creative it took the same kind of artist  to bring that a to of the physicality  and so when I first saw our structures I  practically wept it was I couldn't  believe how beautiful are our dwellings  were and then we we shooting it for like  two days and they pare it down and then  we'd go somewhere and they'd make  something even more beautiful that was  you know Caroline Hanina and ha Nina and  Trish Somerville our customer they all  worked nico Lapage they all worked very  closely with with Steven and with  Francis it was one team really making  that world come to life so that when we  stepped into it you know I was like that  was constantly saying I can't believe  this I can't believe I was too excited  every day when they brought us something  else  a to love costume beautiful I also  walking onto set every day watching them  like tape I was like we're only here for  four days and it's like a whole village  it's so beautiful and big and like  detail-oriented like all of the little  things that we without sight needs to  navigate through our world and I was  thinking there's so much fear that  people will never see there's like no TV  without nails it was lashed together  yeah it would withstand hurricanes  exactly and our tribe the alchemy is  weaving tribe so ropes and and like  twine and things those are that's from  our environment and it's that's kind of  the basis of our tribe so one of the  things that I think little things that I  really enjoyed were the way messages are  passed with the rope you know the the  lettering and it just was so deep and  thought-out see when you were billed  because you're your viewers are watching  it if you establish a world you have to  be true to that world down to the nth  degree and I  production designers and our writers  they that's that's the way it had to be  you know it but it was challenging but  that's why you get into it is to be able  to focus that way completely I heard  there was a boot camp and I would  imagine sometimes you hear about actors  with boot camps and you you know is it  really necessary level bluff but I would  imagine with this one this was very  necessary to learn how to walk to learn  what it would be like without sight can  you sort of talk about the experience of  getting ready for the roles well you  know what I once did a play in several  Croatian back years ago I had three and  a half weeks to learn my script I didn't  speak the language but I was on stage I  learned phonetically what to do so it  would be as if me saying oh I'll be in  that play blast and not speaking a lick  of surface of the Havas key so we could  not come to this work to try to tell  this story with the predicament that  mankind finds itself in six hundred  years in the future if we did not do I  would even call it a boot camp that  sounds like a fitness thing I would call  it no people call it that but I would  call this more of an introduction into  the possibility of a language without  without sight as we know it mm-hmm  completely necessary it was it was  absolutely and I I'm really grateful for  this space that that friend francis  lawrence asked for this time before we  started shooting so that we could get to  know this world and the characters and  the way that we moved about the space  before we even got on to the camera and  that's what i think is so special about  the show is that it feels authentic in  that way what we mainly discovered was a  respect yeah  for people who move about in the world  without vision as we know it as we talk  about it i have to stop there again  congratulations I cannot wait to finish  up the season oh absolutely  I've only seen three as well

